+# Forbidden-move classification table
+
+This table is the **T2 deliverable** of the gomoku development plan
+(`workspace/gomoku/plans/gomoku-r1.md` §7 T2).  It enumerates each
+forbidden-move class — overline, double-four, double-three — by the
+local shape around the placed stone, and the verdict produced by
+`Board.check_forbidden`.  The table is the data source for
+parameterised unit tests in `tests/test_board.py` (testplan UTB-10 to
+UTB-16) and is the contract between the code developer and the test
+plan reviewer (testplan DOC-04).
+
+All examples use `B` for the placed/moved color and `.` for empty
+cells; `#` marks the board edge.  Coordinates are given as offsets
+from the placed stone at index `[2]` of a 5-cell window.
+
+## 1. Overline (long line)
+
+| # | Local 5-cell shape     | Verdict | Reason       |
+|---|------------------------|---------|--------------|
+| 1 | `.BBBB.`               | legal (FIVE) | — |
+| 2 | `.BBBBB.`              | **forbidden** | overline |
+| 3 | `.BBBBBB.`             | **forbidden** | overline |
+| 4 | `BBBB.` (4 stones then off-board) | legal (FIVE) | — |
+| 5 | `BBBBB.` (5 stones then off-board) | **forbidden** | overline |
+
+> Rationale (plan §5.2 + Wikipedia Renju): six or more in a row is
+> forbidden for black.  Exactly five wins regardless of any other
+> shape produced.
+
+## 2. Double-four
+
+A "four" is any pattern with exactly 4 same-colour stones and at least
+one open end that, with one more move, becomes five.
+
+| # | Local 5-cell shape     | Verdict (single direction) | Notes |
+|---|------------------------|----------------------------|-------|
+| 1 | `.XXXX.` (live four)   | counts as a four          | both ends open |
+| 2 | `XXXX.`  (one end off) | counts as a four          | one end open |
+| 3 | `.XXXX#` (other end off) | counts as a four        | one end open  |
+| 4 | `X.XXX`  (broken four) | counts as a four          | one end open |
+| 5 | `XX.XX`  (broken four) | counts as a four          | one end open |
+| 6 | `XXX.X`  (broken four) | counts as a four          | one end open |
+| 7 | `XXXX.`  (closed both) | does NOT count as four    | no open end (closed) |
+
+**Double-four** = ≥ 2 four-counts across the four directions → forbidden.
+
+## 3. Live-three
+
+A "live three" is a three-in-a-row with both ends open, OR a broken
+three (jump) with both ends open.  Per plan §5.2, we also count
+"extension" shapes where the placed stone extends an existing 2-stone
+into a 4-stone with one open end?  **No** — an extension becomes a
+rush four, which is already counted toward double-four.  We only
+count live-three shapes where the placed stone *creates* a 3 with
+both ends open.
+
+| # | Local 5-cell shape     | Counts as live three | Notes |
+|---|------------------------|----------------------|-------|
+| 1 | `.XXX.`  (straight)   | YES | both ends open |
+| 2 | `X.XX.`  (jump right) | YES | fill the gap → `XXXX.` rush four; both ends of the three-stone segment are open |
+| 3 | `.XX.X`  (jump left)  | YES | mirror of (2) |
+| 4 | `XX.X.`  (extension)  | NO (rush four) | already counted toward double-four |
+| 5 | `.X.XX`  (extension)  | NO (rush four) | mirror of (4) |
+| 6 | `X.XXX`  (4 in a row with gap) | NO (rush four) | already counted toward double-four |
+| 7 | `XXX.` (no open end)  | NO | sleep three — not live |
+| 8 | `.XXX#` (one end off) | NO | sleep three — not live |
+
+> The previous (r1-Failed) implementation used a fixed 5-cell window
+> matching only patterns (1) and (3) — and missed extensions where the
+> placed stone sits in front of an existing 2-stone run, e.g.
+> `b.place(7, 7, 'B'); b.place(6, 7, 'B')` then check `(8, 7)` —
+> the resulting `..XXX_` shape has run length 3 with both ends open
+> when the next stone would be placed at `(8, 7)`, but the old
+> window centred on `(8, 7)` was `0, 1, 1, 1, 0` only by lucky
+> arrangement — the actual run-length + open-ends logic is the
+> correct implementation.
+
+**Double-three** = ≥ 2 live-three counts across the four directions →
